  "comment": "**What it does**\nQuality compounders among **banks and insurers** - the group `quality_compounders` excludes on purpose, judged by THEIR rules instead of ordinary-company rules.\n\n**Why a separate screen**: margins, ROIC, current ratio and debt/EBITDA are meaningless for a bank. Debt is their raw material, not a risk to minimise. So this screen leans on ROE, book value and price-to-book.\n\n- **Quality, their way** - ROE 12%+.\n- **Not just leverage** - equity multiplier under 15. Financials are structurally leveraged (the median here is about 8.9x), so this only cuts the true outliers - the ones whose high ROE is borrowed rather than earned.\n- **Compounding, their way** - book value growing 7%+ a year, because retained earnings compounding into book value IS how a financial compounds. Forward EPS growth is the fallback when book-value history is thin.\n- **Not overpriced** - P/B at or below 2.0, OR below its industry median.\n- **Proven** - above the 200-day line, RS Rank 50+.\n\n**The two numbers you left open**: 'reasonable' P/B and 'decent' RS Rank were mine to pick. **P/B 2.0 and RS 50** give **41 names**. P/B is the sensitive one - at 1.5 the list drops to 21, at 2.5 it rises to 58.\n\n**How to tweak**\n- **P/B is your main dial** - it does the heaviest cutting (117 down to 36 at 1.5).\n- **Want deep value?** Set `pb <= 1.0` for banks trading below book, but check WHY each one is there.\n- **Want higher quality?** Raise `roe` to 15.\n- **Want the most conservative balance sheets?** Tighten `equity_multiplier` to 10.\n- **Note**: `book_value_cagr_3y` is the metric that matters most here. If you loosen one thing, do not loosen that one.\n\n**How to sort for best picks**\n- **Sort by `quality_score`** - the thesis. For financials it leans on ROE and ROA, which is the right axis.\n- **Tiebreak on `momentum_score`** - confirmation the market agrees.\n- **Worth a look**: sort by `book_value_cagr_3y` descending for the purest reading of financial compounding.\n- **Avoid `value_score`** - it leans on sales and cash-flow multiples that are hidden for financials, so it has less to work with here than it looks.",
  "ai_instructions": "Find quality compounders among banks and insurers \u2014 the group my\nquality_compounders screen excludes on purpose, judged by THEIR rules: return on\nequity and growing book value, not margins and current ratios.\n- **Goal**: long-term growth in financials. **Kinds**: banks and insurance only.\n- **Quality, their way**: ROE 12%+ (without crazy leverage doing the work).\n- **Compounding, their way**: book value growing ~7%+ a year (earnings retained\n  into book is how financials compound); forward earnings growth as a fallback.\n- **Not overpriced**: price-to-book reasonable or below industry peers.\n- **Proven**: above the 200-day line, decent RS Rank.\n- **Dividends**: ignore for this screen. **Size**: $1B and bigger. **Risk**: moderate.\n- **Results**: ~20-150 names. **Sorting**: quality first, tiebreak momentum.",
